    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel.jpg" alt="Vallabhbhai Patel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Vallabhbhai Patel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sardar_patel_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Government of India work, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When British rule ended in August 1947, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a> consisted of some two dozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> and chiefships, among them ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> kingdoms such as Mewar (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>), Marwar (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> and Bikaner, together with the small British province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer-merwara">Ajmer-Merwara</a>. Their rulers had signed Instruments of Accession surrendering defence, foreign affairs and communications to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, but the States Ministry under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Vallabhbhai Patel</a>, with its secretary V.P. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/v-p-menon">Menon</a>, judged most of them too small to survive as separate units and pressed them to merge. The union was built in stages. On 25 March 1948 nine states of south-eastern Rajputana, including Kota, Bundi, Jhalawar, Tonk, Banswara, Dungarpur, Partabgarh, Kishangarh and Shahpura, with the chiefships of Kushalgarh and Lawa, formed a union with its capital at Kota. Udaipur, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhupal-singh-of-udaipur">Maharana Bhupal Singh</a> had at first hoped to lead a union of his own, joined on 18 April 1948, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> inaugurated the enlarged United State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, with Udaipur as capital. On 30 March 1949, after long negotiations, the four great states of Jaipur, Jodhpur, Bikaner and Jaisalmer joined to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-state-of-greater-rajasthan">United State of Greater Rajasthan</a>, inaugurated by Patel at Jaipur, a date still celebrated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan-day">Rajasthan Day</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kolkata"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kolkata.jpg" alt="Kolkata" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_maidan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NalGup20, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The union covered most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a> and the Aravalli hills, a dry region where farming depended on an uncertain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> and famine was a recurring memory. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1951 the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> had about 16 million people, most of them villagers growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a>, with wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> in the better-watered east and south, while herders kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and goats across the desert. Literacy was below one in ten, among the lowest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Land was held largely under the <em>jagirdari</em> system, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> nobles, the jagirdars and thakurs, enjoyed revenue rights and judicial powers over their villages, and peasants paid heavy rents and customary levies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> and other tribal communities of the southern hills lived largely outside the cash economy. Yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a> was also the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the Marwari merchant communities, whose firms, such as those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birla-family">Birla</a> and Bajaj families, had become leading industrial houses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and beyond. Popular politics had grown through the <em>praja mandals</em>, the people's associations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mewar-praja-mandal">Mewar Praja Mandal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manikya-lal-varma">Manikya Lal Varma</a> and the Marwar Lok Parishad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-narayan-vyas">Jai Narayan Vyas</a>, which pressed for responsible government and an end to feudal dues.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi.jpg" alt="Mahatma Gandhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahatma-Gandhi,_studio,_1931.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> never had an army of its own, since defence had passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> under the Instruments of Accession. Its member states, however, brought with them the Indian State Forces maintained under British supervision, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">Sardar</a> Risala, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> infantry and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganga-risala">Ganga Risala</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> corps of Bikaner, which had served in both world wars. Under the covenants of merger these units passed to the union and were then taken over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a>, which absorbed the State Forces into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> by 1951. The union also faced serious problems of order. The partition of 1947 had brought violence to the eastern states, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meos">Meo Muslims</a> in Alwar and Bharatpur were attacked and driven out, and after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> in January 1948 the Government of India took over the administration of Alwar while its ruler was investigated; Alwar and Bharatpur then entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsya-union">Matsya Union</a>, which joined Rajasthan in May 1949. The western states of Jaisalmer, Bikaner and Jodhpur gave the union a long desert frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, policed after merger by a unified state police. Bandits, or dacoits, in the ravines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambal-river">Chambal</a> valley and resistance by some jagirdars to the loss of their privileges remained recurrent security concerns.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-ii-of-jaipur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/man-singh-ii-of-jaipur.jpg" alt="Man Singh II of Jaipur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-ii-of-jaipur">Man Singh II of Jaipur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Man_Singh_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bassano Ltd, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The union was a federation of former princes held together by covenants negotiated with the States Ministry. Each ruler surrendered his powers of government in return for a tax-free privy purse, the guarantee of his titles and private property, and the right of succession. The highest offices went to the senior princes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhupal-singh-of-udaipur">Bhupal Singh of Udaipur</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> in April 1948 and, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-state-of-greater-rajasthan">Greater Rajasthan</a>, Maharajpramukh for life, a unique dignity reflecting Mewar's precedence, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-ii-of-jaipur">Man Singh II of Jaipur</a> became Rajpramukh and constitutional head of the state. Real executive power lay with a council of ministers drawn from the popular movements: Gokul Lal Asawa headed the first government at Kota, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manikya-lal-varma">Manikya Lal Varma</a> the government formed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiralal-shastri">Hiralal Shastri</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praja-mandal">Praja Mandal</a> became the first premier of Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> in 1949, though the transitional governments were not yet elected. The merger of more than twenty administrations, each with its own laws, revenue systems, courts and currencies of account, was a large task. To balance local pride, Jaipur became the capital while the High Court, inaugurated in August 1949, was placed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, and other offices were distributed among former capitals. The States Ministry closely supervised the new government's finances and appointments throughout.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rambagh-palace"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rambagh-palace.jpg" alt="Rambagh Palace" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rambagh-palace">Rambagh Palace</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rambagh_Palace_hotel_Jaipur_lobby_courtyard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chainwit., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> was one of the clearest examples of the integration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, the process by which more than five hundred states were absorbed into the Indian Union between 1947 and 1950 with little bloodshed. Its stepwise construction, from a small union of minor states to a large unit absorbing the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> kingdoms, showed how the States Ministry combined persuasion, financial guarantees and pressure from popular movements to bring proud dynasties into a democratic framework. The union ended a political order that had lasted, in some states, for more than a thousand years, and replaced it with a single administration, a common legal system and, from 1952, elected legislators. Its result, the state of Rajasthan, became after the 1956 reorganisation one of the largest states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and, since the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 2000, the largest by area. The former rulers did not disappear: many entered politics, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gayatri-devi">Gayatri Devi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a> in 1962, and several turned palaces into hotels, starting with Jaipur's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rambagh-palace">Rambagh Palace</a> in 1957, helping to make Rajasthan's royal heritage the basis of one of India's main tourist industries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan-day">Rajasthan Day</a> on 30 March commemorates the union's decisive stage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ajmer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajmer.jpg" alt="Ajmer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ADHAI_DIN_KA_JHONPRA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SUDHIR UPADHYAY JI, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> did not decline but was completed and transformed into a regular state of the Indian Union. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsya-union">Matsya Union</a> of Alwar, Bharatpur, Dholpur and Karauli joined on 15 May 1949, the last remaining state, Sirohi, was divided: on 26 January 1950, the day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a> came into force, most of Sirohi was merged into the union, but Abu and Delwara, with the hill station of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-abu">Mount Abu</a>, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>. On the same day the union became the state of Rajasthan, one of the Part B states of the new republic, which were former princely unions headed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> and placed for some years under the general control of the central government. Elections in 1952 gave Rajasthan its first elected assembly. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-reorganisation-act-1956">States Reorganisation Act</a> of 1956 completed the process: from 1 November 1956 Abu and Delwara, the Part C state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, formerly the British province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer-merwara">Ajmer-Merwara</a>, and the Sunel Tappa enclave were added, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sironj">Sironj</a> subdivision went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, and the office of Rajpramukh was abolished in favour of an appointed governor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagirs</a> were resumed under legislation of 1952, and the privy purses guaranteed in the covenants were abolished by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-sixth-amendment-of-the-constitution-of-india">Twenty-sixth Amendment</a> in 1971.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The United State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> matters as the framework through which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> and Bikaner, surrendered their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and merged between 1948 and 1950, a showcase of the peaceful integration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> and the origin of India's largest state, Rajasthan.</em></p>

